derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn) writes ...

        :
>Now, a CD [mono] does NOT use 44kbits/sec!  It uses a 44khz sampling
>rate, but each sample is 16 bits: about 706 kbits/sec.  For low-grade
>sound, 8-bit samples are adequate.

Huh? Lessee. The maximum CD running time is c. 76 minutes. 76 minutes
x 60 seconds gives 4560 seconds. 4560 x (706k/8) gives 402,420,000
bytes. CD-ROMS store about 550 Mbytes. Hmm. I vaguely recall -- now
you poke me -- that the samples aren't 16 bits; they're something
weird like 12 bits, and the 16-bit DACs are using some oversampling
mechanism to improve their fidelity.

And now for somethhing different ...

>> The low-capacity tapes ICI showed could store something like 50Gb, and tapes
>> the size of a VHS cartridge could handle on the order of 1-2 Tbytes.

>Now this is a whole different ballgame.  I did not realize that there
>were commercially available terabyte "tape" cartridges.  Could you
>tell us more about this?  

Well, my memory's a bit hazy. Digital Paper (I _think_ that is what
they TM'd it as) got exposed in Byte, New Scientist, and similar
places about 3-5 years ago then went away quietly. I remember seeing
photographs of a variety of media, including discs and tapes ranging
in size from 4mm DAT sized up to huge 12" spools. The concept was to
market cheap archive storage with a cost of c. .1 cent/Mbyte. (i.e.
$10 for a 10 Gbyte tape).

I presume though that the main market for such a technology is for
high-capacity backup servers hanging off mainframes and enterprise
systems; not being a mainframe owner I don't get the kind of sales
literature that would contain ads for it.

All of my objections go away if these
>are in fact feasible; in fact, LOTS of objections go away in this
>case!  Such a tape could hold many hundreds of hours of video!
>The complete genomes of thousands of people (actually, many more
>with a differential encoding)!  Extropians archives for nearly a month!

>> the storage requirement is within the reach of an organization with a
>> budget on the order of the hundreds of millions available to NSA or
>> GCHQ. 

>Well, I maintain that today it's infeasible to get much below 20kbits/sec
>per phone line without a dedicated fast DSP per conversation (meaning a
>million DSPs or so for all the phone conversations, too expensive today).
>Given that, this gives roughly 8,000 terabytes per month.  With a
>budget of $8M/month (a hundred million per year, imaginable), this
>scenario becomes feasible if and when storage can be gotten for
>$1000/terabyte.  If those new devices you mention are in fact in
>production (ooh, future shock, I love it!), that would be possible.
>For DAT tapes, we're still off by an order of magnitude.

Given a storage cost of $1/Gbyte, we can cost out a monitoring
operation at that $100m/year. Note that GCHQ has a budget >80 million
UK pounds/year, and NSA (from my hazy memory of "Inside the Puzzle
Palace") is something stratospheric (like $5Bn).

Back to my main point, though. Traffic analysis is _definitely_ within
the reach of agency budgets today. Total phone network logging is
_possibly_ within their budget, and mass tapping ( >1 million phones
permanently monitored) is _definitely_ within budget.

Question: suppose the government -- any government -- were to clamp down
suddenly on electronic networking by subversives (i.e.  most of us on
this list), TOMORROW, what would be the most survivable network
mechanism? What SHOULD we have in place? And why DON'T we have a
tamper-proof network that is invulnerable to cryptanalysis,
steganalysis, traffic analysis, and plain old-fashioned
boot-through-door confiscation?

WARNING: maybe I'm paranoid, but it might not be a good idea to post
to the list any plaintext that you would object to the guys in black
hats reading. After all, we're talking about black-hat proof networks
here ...



-- Charlie


