Date: Thu, 25 Nov 93 13:03:59 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Comments on NSA

Fortuneately, mere taping does not constitute monitoring.  Ever try
recovering a major system from several tape backups?  Arggghh, may
I never have to do that again!  Searching a 1,000-tape library
organized by time and region for specific vocal patterns or
keywords would be very expensive, unless the wiretappers already know 
the time and place of the call to be searched.  Global searches,
traffic analysis, etc. have to be done in batch runs through the
tapes with search times comparable to real time (divided by the
amount they can do in parallel).  It might be more convenient to 
just do such global runs in real time.  Most searches that require
cross-correlation of large amounts of data between tapes
(ie some kinds of traffic analysis, depending on the method
of mass-wiretapping) would be prohibitively expensive.

Complete mass-wiretap of the U.S. would also require either (a) collusion 
by all the Baby Bells, GTE, etc. or (b) monitoring all major microwave and
satellite links.  The resulting data would be extremely disorganized --
encoded in a wide variety of ways, redundant, etc.  They could
only hope to search those links using the most common encoding
schemes, and traffic analysis between two such regimes might be
quite difficult.   An atypical modem compression scheme would be
practically equivalent to encryption for the purposes of such 
automated searches.

