>Institute were radio intercept operators, 15% were POW interogators,
>and the remaining 15% were officers about to be assigned overseas
>and spooks.
>
>The vast majority studied Russian, German, Chinese, and Korean (in
>approximate descending order).

My Dad went through this.  Graduated from high school at 17 with no
plans, so Grandpa, who was allegedly "sargeant-major in charge of NATO
forces in Europe, the highest-ranking enlisted man in the US Army" pulled
a few strings to get Dad into Army Intelligence in spite of his dry skin
condition, which would've kept him from passing the physical, and he
got sent to Monterey to study Russian, then in Japan to translate radio
messages from the USSR.

According to what he told me (never can tell how reliable it was - he was
perversely deceptive, for one thing, and claimed that he couldn't tell
some stuff because it was classified for another thing), they had him
studying Russian 8 hours a day, 5 days a week, for about 3 months, if I
recall correctly.  Note the difference between this teaching method and
the 50-minute hours, single subject per hour method prevalent in public
school.  Evidently, if they want you to learn it, they do it the former way,
not the latter.

Benefits: he knew Russian pretty well, as far as I could tell, able to do
such miraculous things as correct the English subtitles in Russian-language
movies, get us around in Yugoslavia without being able to speak any of the
indigenous languages (and get Russian-hating students mad at us while he was
at it), and had stories to tell about barrack life in Monterey and Japan
(he picked up some Japanese, too, which, combined with "Shogun," is probably
where I got what little I wakarimas).  Evidently, when they got off duty at
the end of the day, they'd walk into town, buy a cheap jug of Gallo wine,
and walk back to the base, drinking it on the way.  Dad also claimed to know
a fellow there who could drink beer so fast that he'd never stop pissing
until he ran out of it to drink.  All I remember 'bout Japan is that he picked
up a taste for sushi.

Costs: he got banned from going to the USSR by both the USA and the USSR; the
USA didn't want him turning sides, and the USSR didn't want him infiltrating
to kill their Premier, a la the movie "Spies Like Us" :-).

>          Jeff

