If we include CompuServe et. al. access to stock quotes in today's
net commerce, we must also include the flourishing net porn industry 
on BBS's.  BBS's take in upwards of $1 billion a year (according to 
_Wired_) and I'd estimate that porn boards make up a majority of that 
figure.  

Despite the many customers who seem to disregard their own privacy
with controversial services such as porn, I suspect many of the more
sophisticated customers would pay quite a bit extra for secure
privacy.  Here's a very simple system: the BBS can accept
money orders enclosed with a nickname and password
created by the customer.  These can then be used to
access the telnet account based on that nickname.  The
local service account is then then debited by using the services 
and credited by mailing more money orders with the pass phrase.

This system is far more secure than credit cards, which are
notoriously subject to fraud even without the rich new
possibilities for credit card abuse created by the Internet.
The customer need trust the distant net service with a small 
money order, not access to their entire credit line.  The
business need not make the unrealistic expectation that
a net account corresponds to a "real name".

The money order and password might be instead sent
to a debit Billing Service that maintains accounts for a wide 
variety of online services, along the lines of Phil Trubey's 
proposal.  Small account credits can be used to "ping"
the various online services, and the fraudulent services
quickly unmasked.

This money order system also has the quality that is vital
for international, decentralized Internet commerce --
it is _jurisdiction independent_.  Small business cannot
afford the massive amounts of legal help needed to sort out
the various means of credit enforcement in the vast number
of jurisdictions criss-crossed by the net.  They must instead
use transactions that rely on no such enforcement.  This is
a major but necessary change in outlook if small business is
to take its place beside the megacorps in multinational
commerce.  Due to the same lack of legal budget, smart
small businesses on the net will also take security 
steps, such as encryption and foiling traffic analysis, to minimize 
the consequence of accidentally violating obscure entries in a library 
full of regulations somewhere on the other side of the planet.
Anybody who thinks that small business should instead
hire lawyers from all over the planet to be sure that they
satisfy every jot and tittle in every principality, has obviously 
never run a small business. That isn't even feasible across the 50 
states of the U.S.!  A good IMP should be as useful in and
between Europe, South Africa, Japan, and Singapore as it is in 
and between the states of the U.S. or EC.

I suspect most Internet commerce in the near term is going 
to be a straightforward migration from the OSI/X.*00 world and the 
BBS world, not new services made out of whole cloth. 
Pure-info publishing (books, CDs, etc.) is going to face severe 
problems with piracy (as digital porn does now).  Over the long
term, many more services will be possible from the emerging net-based
multinational small business.  Since small business is 
increasing majority share of the world economy, the development
of jurisdiction-independent protocols, from the simple (money
order activated net accounts) to the sophisticated (online
and offline digital cash) for multinational small
business may well be the most lucrative business opportunity
of the decade.  Jurisdictional independence looks like the
keystone of the new generation of net commerce.

