Subject: Librarians

Perry, quoted by Kennita:

   LIBRARIANS. Most people think of librarians as being the old women who
   sit around and answer small children's questions on how to use card
   catalogues -- few people realize that what librarians actually worry
   about is this very problem -- the indexing of all human knowledge.

Since I've been working with all these Info & Library Science professors,
I'll make a comment before dvdt jumps in.  I do have a fair amount of
respect for librarians (Library Scientists?), especially the reference 
types.  But they've all given up on "the indexing of all human knowledge"
a few years ago.  Or, more precisely, they've given up on indexing it 
all by hand (because there's no way to keep up and there are a lot of
retrieval problems) and some of them are working on automated indexing.

Systems exist for indexing knowledge, but they don't work all that well.
Most people have about 5 avenues they will try before going to the
library to find a bit of knowledge, and those avenues involve asking 
other people who they think have the knowledge stored in their brain.
There are lots of hard problems, and indexing is one of them.

