Subject: Indexing

   every once in a while, people would
   find that their system for indexing had become so unsatisfactory
   that some of them would feel a strong impulse to reshuffle everything.

   It would be like fights about splitting groups on the net, only
   much, much worse. And, even though it would be possible to use
   computers to keep both systems of indexing going at the same time,
   they'd represent different worldviews and take time to learn, so
   that people would be fighting for their preferred indexing.

Clifford Neuman's Virtual System Model is a good solution for the
problem of maintaining multiple indexes to information.

It allows a collection of information to have as many different
hierarchically-organized name spaces as desired.  Each name space is
potentially independant (although name spaces may be combined if
desired), allowing individual users to name and organize things as
they see fit.  Plus, it also offers neat features like algorithmically
generated name spaces.

For more info, some Postscript-format papers concerning the Prospero
system (Neuman's current implementation of the VSM) can be found in
the directory ftp://prospero.isi.edu/pub/papers/prospero/
Source code is in pub/prospero on the same machine.

